.TH WORDEXP 3
.SH NAME
wordexp, wordfree \- perform word expansion like a POSIX shell
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <wordexp.h>
.PP
.BI "int wordexp(const char *" words ", wordexp_t *" we ", int " flags ");"
.br
.BI "void wordfree(wordexp_t *" we ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B wordexp()
function performs the word expansions a shell applies to a command line on the
string
.IR words ,
returning the resulting list of words in the structure pointed to by
.IR we .
The expansions are performed in order:
.IP \(bu 3
.B Tilde expansion:
a leading
.B ~
becomes the value of
.BR $HOME ,
and
.BI ~ user
becomes that user's home directory (looked up with
.BR getpwnam (3)).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Parameter expansion:
.BI $ name
and
.BI ${ name }
are replaced with the value of the environment variable
.IR name .
The special parameter
.B $$
expands to the process ID.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Field splitting:
unquoted runs of whitespace (space, tab, newline) split the input \(em and the
result of an unquoted parameter expansion \(em into separate words.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Pathname expansion:
each resulting field is passed through
.BR glob (3);
a field that matches no pathname is taken literally.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Quote removal:
single quotes quote everything literally, double quotes suppress field and
pathname expansion but still allow parameter expansion, and a backslash escapes
the next character outside single quotes.
.PP
The
.I we
structure is defined in
.I <wordexp.h>
as:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
typedef struct {
    size_t   we_wordc;   /* count of expanded words */
    char   **we_wordv;   /* the NULL-terminated word vector */
    size_t   we_offs;    /* slots reserved at the front of we_wordv */
} wordexp_t;
.EE
.RE
.PP
The
.I flags
argument is the bitwise OR of zero or more of:
.TP
.B WRDE_DOOFFS
Reserve
.I we->we_offs
leading NULL slots at the head of
.IR we_wordv .
.TP
.B WRDE_APPEND
Append the words to the vector from a previous call rather than allocating a new
one.
.TP
.B WRDE_NOCMD
Fail with
.B WRDE_CMDSUB
if command substitution is requested.
.TP
.B WRDE_REUSE
The
.I we
argument was passed to a previous successful call;
.BR wordfree ()
it before reuse.
.TP
.B WRDE_SHOWERR
Do not suppress shell error output (no effect in this implementation).
.TP
.B WRDE_UNDEF
Fail with
.B WRDE_BADVAL
if an undefined variable is referenced.
.PP
The
.B wordfree()
function frees the storage allocated by a successful
.BR wordexp ()
call.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success
.B wordexp()
returns 0 and fills in
.IR we .
On failure it returns one of the nonzero error codes below and leaves
.I we
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B WRDE_NOSPACE
Out of memory.
.I (Note:
on this error
.I we_wordv
may have been partially updated.)
.TP
.B WRDE_BADCHAR
.IR words
contains an unquoted shell metacharacter:
one of
.BR \(rs| ,
.BR & ,
.BR ; ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR { ,
or
.BR } .
.TP
.B WRDE_BADVAL
An undefined variable was referenced and
.B WRDE_UNDEF
was set.
.TP
.B WRDE_CMDSUB
Command substitution
.RB ( `...` " or " $(...) )
was encountered.
This implementation never executes command substitution, so it reports this
error whenever command substitution appears, as a shell does when
.B WRDE_NOCMD
is set.
.TP
.B WRDE_SYNTAX
Shell syntax error, such as unbalanced quotes.
.SH NOTES
Command substitution and arithmetic expansion are not performed.
Arbitrary shell word splitting on a custom
.B IFS
is not supported; the field separators are always space, tab, and newline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Expand a configuration-file path that may contain a tilde or environment
variable.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <wordexp.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    wordexp_t we;
    if (wordexp("~/.config/*.conf", &we, 0) == 0) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < we.we_wordc; i++)
            printf("%s\(rsn", we.we_wordv[i]);
        wordfree(&we);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR glob (3),
.BR getpwnam (3),
.BR getenv (3),
.BR fnmatch (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008.
This implementation supports a practical subset (no command/arithmetic
substitution, no custom
.BR IFS ).
